WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:06.800
Elon Musk may be the superstar CEO of SpaceX&nbsp;
but a lot of the credit goes to the guy who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.240
instrumental in making the rockets fly. I'm Tom&nbsp;
Mueller, I'm a co-founder of the company. Tom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.280
Mueller may not be famous like Musk but he's a big&nbsp;
part of the reason the company is as successful as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.480
it is today. As Vice-President of Propulsion and&nbsp;
later the Propulsion Chief Technology Officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.000
he led the design, testing, and construction of&nbsp;
SpaceX's engines. Before joining SpaceX, Mueller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.560
developed engines at TRW, a large aerospace company&nbsp;
in Los Angeles later acquired by Northrup Grumman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.280
As a hobby, he spent weekends testing and&nbsp;
flying his own machines in the Mojave desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.400
with an amateur rocket club called Reaction&nbsp;
Research Society. That's how he met Musk almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.520
20 years ago through the club. The future SpaceX&nbsp;
CEO had been looking to recruit a bright team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:56.320
of engineers to build rockets. The year before, Musk&nbsp;
had gone to Moscow to try to buy refurbished ICBMs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.520
but when the Russians wanted too much money, he set&nbsp;
out to build his own rocket for less. So when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.640
came time to founding SpaceX, Musk saw in Mueller&nbsp;
someone who knew everything there was to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:13.200
about rocket engines. When I met Elon it's like, wow&nbsp;
I'd really like to go do this thing. It's high risk but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.960
it's where I want to be. Going to a startup was&nbsp;
risky but also gave him free reign to do more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.880
Mueller is known for developing rocket engines&nbsp;
from scratch including the Merlin engine. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.680
is a 95,000-pound thrust engine. Basically this&nbsp;
engine will burn a hole right through concrete.&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.120
These engines power the Falcon line of rockets.&nbsp;
The 27 in the Falcon Heavy generate over 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.480
million pounds of thrust at liftoff. And the&nbsp;
Falcon 9 gets its name from the nine Merlin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.120
engines in the first stage. They're fueled&nbsp;
by rocket-grade kerosene and liquid oxygen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.480
It's not easy developing an engine&nbsp;
that can withstand a 170-second burn&nbsp;-&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.400
long enough to send a payload into orbit and then&nbsp;
return safely to Earth. Merlin revolutionized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.560
space flight as it's designed to be reused.&nbsp;
Building reusable rockets put SpaceX on the map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.240
It's now a common sight to see the Falcon 9 first&nbsp;
stage perform one of its signature landings. Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:13.520
year, that rocket launched astronauts aboard the&nbsp;
Crew Dragon to the International Space Station.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:19.040
Mueller also developed the engines that helped&nbsp;
the Dragon spacecraft maneuver while in orbit. Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.240
before these achievements, Mueller was right beside&nbsp;
Musk during the company's most difficult days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.160
when attempts to launch its first rocket, the&nbsp;
Falcon 1, kept failing. The third time it tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.040
to fly into orbit, the first stage rear-ended&nbsp;
the second caused by a small amount of residual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.320
thrust from the first stage after the engine was&nbsp;
cut off. SpaceX had enough money to scrounge up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.600
fourth and what would have been final flight if&nbsp;
it failed. This time, the team told the rocket to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.200
wait a few more seconds after the engine was cut&nbsp;
off before stage separation. Fourth time's a charm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.520
The Falcon 1 became the first privately&nbsp;
developed liquid fuel rocket to orbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.000
the planet which led to a billion-dollar&nbsp;
contract with NASA that saved the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.600
Mueller has been an integral part of the story&nbsp;
of SpaceX from the start but he almost never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.360
became an engineer if it were not for some sage&nbsp;
advice. As the son of a logging truck driver raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.920
in a small town in Idaho, being an engineer&nbsp;
wasn't something he considered. He wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.200
be an aircraft mechanic. Then I took a math class&nbsp;
when I was a freshman in high school and my math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.560
teacher said to me, "Do you want to be the guy that&nbsp;
fixes a plane or the guy that designs the plane."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.880
That was a pivotal point of my life. Of course I&nbsp;
wanted to be the guy that designs the plane. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.920
one had told me that I had the aptitude to be an&nbsp;
engineer. I didn't even know what an engineer did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.680
That teacher changed my life. He spent summers&nbsp;
logging to pay his way through college studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.860
mechanical engineering at the University of Idaho.&nbsp;

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.621
After graduation, he set out for California and&nbsp;
its thriving aerospace industry but instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.621 --> 00:03:59.541
of finding a job designing planes, he wanted to&nbsp;
work on his childhood passion: rockets. I realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.541 --> 00:04:06.251
that that was a key decision. A fork in the road&nbsp;
where I made the right choice to do what I love.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.251 --> 00:04:10.811
Don't settle. Put yourself on the right&nbsp;
path and end up where you want to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.811 --> 00:04:16.891
After a storied career at SpaceX, he stepped&nbsp;
back into a part-time advisory role in 2016&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.891 --> 00:04:22.338
as Musk in the propulsion department led the&nbsp;
development of new engines for a new rocket.

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:29.949
This could be the first engine to take people&nbsp;
to Mars. Raptor engines power SpaceX's fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.949 --> 00:04:35.309
reusable rocket, Starship. They're fueled&nbsp;
by liquid oxygen and liquid methane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.309 --> 00:04:40.349
Methane could possibly be made on the red&nbsp;
planet for the journey home. As SpaceX soars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.349 --> 00:04:45.549
to new heights, much of its achievements can be&nbsp;
traced back to the early work of Tom Mueller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.549 --> 00:04:51.869
In fact, Musk acknowledged his contributions were&nbsp;
critical to SpaceX's success. The man who helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.869 --> 00:04:57.389
make the rockets fly officially retired in late&nbsp;
2020, capping off a nearly two-decade career at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.389 --> 00:05:04.829
SpaceX which has been, in his words, "quite a ride".&nbsp;
Ability plus luck equals success. If you have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.829 --> 00:05:09.869
ability then luck is your opportunity. Mueller&nbsp;
may have met the right visionary at the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.869 --> 00:05:16.429
time but it took more than luck to win Musk over. A
great engineer requires a solid background in math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

